WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.590
Secure HTTP clients begin with mature building blocks.

2
00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:13.015
Use maintained libraries or approved platform APIs, keep TLS certificate and hostname validation enabled, and document the trust-store and endpoint

3
00:00:13.015 --> 00:00:13.913
identity model.

4
00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:17.952
Treat development trust settings as separate from production policy.

5
00:00:17.952 --> 00:00:19.298
Build requests deliberately.

6
00:00:19.298 --> 00:00:23.338
Use structured APIs for URLs, headers, bodies, and encodings.

7
00:00:23.338 --> 00:00:26.479
Keep trusted configuration separate from external input.

8
00:00:26.479 --> 00:00:33.660
Validate any user-controlled destination before use, and make redirect, proxy, and credential-forwarding behavior explicit.

9
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:35.006
Protect authentication material.

10
00:00:35.006 --> 00:00:43.982
API keys, bearer tokens, cookies, client certificates, private keys, and signed request material should come from approved secret mechanisms, have

11
00:00:43.982 --> 00:00:48.470
narrow scope, and stay out of logs and support artifacts.

12
00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:52.958
Plan expiration, rotation, and environment separation before the first incident.

13
00:00:52.958 --> 00:00:57.446
Treat responses as untrusted input and operations as bounded work.

14
00:00:57.446 --> 00:01:00.588
Validate status, content type, shape, and meaning.

15
00:01:00.588 --> 00:01:05.076
Limit response size, decompression, buffering, redirects, retries, and total duration.

16
00:01:05.076 --> 00:01:15.398
Log enough safe context to operate the system, and review HTTP client code as the place where network behavior, credentials, parsing, and reliability

17
00:01:15.398 --> 00:01:15.847
meet.

18
00:01:15.847 --> 00:01:23.477
These habits keep outbound integrations understandable as services, environments, dependencies, and security requirements evolve across the lifetime

19
00:01:23.477 --> 00:01:29.760
of the application and its supporting infrastructure in production systems and team ownership records.
